{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem spoke, etc., saying: Avenge, etc.</b> It is difficult: if the utterance came to say to Israel, as is heard from the word ״לֵאמֹר,״ it should have said ״נִקְמוּ נִקְמַתְכֶם מֵאֵת״ etc. And in the Midrash Tanchuma they said, and this is their language: ״וַיְדַבֵּר״ means nothing but a term of harshness, etc.; Moses began to appease the Holy One, blessed be He, regarding his death, but He was not appeased, etc. Moses said to Him: ״I am worthy of death, for I have seen wonders,״ etc. And HaShem appeased him with two things: ״If you wish to live many years, Israel will not see the downfall of their enemies, and you will not conquer Midian before them,״ etc. And once Moses was appeased, it was hard before the Holy One, blessed be He, that He had decreed death upon him, etc. — end quote. Behold, the author of the midrash was sensitive to what we examined, and according to his words the verse is properly resolved. ״וַיְדַבֵּר״ in the sense of harshness — this alludes to two kinds of harshness: one upon Moses regarding his death, and one in the eyes of HaShem over having decreed death upon His beloved. And the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> is intended to allude to the appeasement that HaShem offered Moses, to induce him to be appeased to die, on account of ״נְקֹם״ etc., as stated in the midrash. Or it may mean by the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> to inform of Moses' desire — that Moses desired of HaShem not to die, and HaShem answered him ״נְקֹם״ etc., meaning: in order that He may avenge the enemies of Israel." "<b>And according to</b> the plain sense of the verse, it will be explained in this manner: ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳״ — ״וַיְדַבֵּר״ in the sense of speech, meaning He spoke to him a word of sovereign authority, like all the other commandments in the Torah. And His saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b> according to its plain sense — to say to Israel, like every ״לֵאמֹר״ stated in the Torah; and here it needs to be said more than in any other place, because He said ״נְקֹם,״ one might conclude that he alone would carry out the vengeance according to the plain sense of the statement, and this is not far-fetched, for he was a mighty warrior and HaShem was his help. Go and learn from Jonathan son of Saul, etc. (I Samuel 14), and all the more so from Moses, the mightiest of the mighty. Therefore He said to him: say it to Israel. And His saying <b>נְקֹם</b> etc., meaning: because HaShem made his death contingent upon the vengeance against Midian, as it is written ״אַחַר תֵּאָסֵף״ etc., He said to him that in his communicating the matter to them, by this the verse imputes to him that he is the avenger, for he literally surrenders his soul for the matter — for even though he knows that by so doing he will die, nevertheless he will say to Israel what is stated immediately below. There is no fulfillment of the commandment of ״בְּכָל נַפְשְׁךָ״ like this, and below it will be explained in another manner."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:06.754321986Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}